\chapter*{Unit 3B: Applications of Integration}
\addcontentsline{toc}{chapter}{Unit 3B: Applications of Integration}

Unit 3A built the integral as accumulated change and developed methods for computing it. Unit 3B uses that machinery in standard geometric, physical, and quantitative applications. The emphasis is not on collecting exotic formulas. It is on constructing the correct small contribution, integrating over the correct variable, and interpreting the result with units.

\section*{Unit map}
\begin{enumerate}
\item Area between curves
\item Volumes by slicing, disks, washers, shells, and known cross-sections
\item Arc length and surface area
\item Density, mass, moments, and center of mass
\item Work, springs, pumping, and fluid pressure
\item Accumulation from marginal quantities and probability densities
\item Review, modeling practice, and two practice exams
\end{enumerate}

\begin{bgconcept}[title={The application template}]
Nearly every application follows the same structure:
\[
\text{total}=\lim\sum(\text{local contribution})=\int(\text{contribution density})\,d(\text{input}).
\]
The hard part is choosing the contribution correctly. Once the model is right, the calculus is often routine.
\end{bgconcept}

\section{Area Between Curves}
\lessonobjective{Set up and evaluate area integrals using top-minus-bottom or right-minus-left.}

\begin{bgexposition}[title={Area is built from the length of a representative slice}]
For a vertical slice, the local height of a region is top minus bottom. Multiplying that height by a small width \(dx\) gives a thin rectangle whose area approximates the corresponding piece of the region. Integrating adds these pieces. The formula is therefore not a slogan to memorize but a direct description of the geometry of one slice.

The first task is to find intersections and determine which curve is on top over each interval. If the order changes, the setup must be split. A negative result is a warning that the curves were subtracted in the wrong order, because geometric area cannot be negative. Sketching the region and drawing one labeled slice is usually faster than repairing an integral assembled blindly.
\end{bgexposition}


For vertical slices,
\[
A=\int_a^b[\text{top}-\text{bottom}]dx.
\]
For horizontal slices,
\[
A=\int_c^d[\text{right}-\text{left}]dy.
\]

\begin{bgguided}[title={Area enclosed by a line and parabola}]
Find the area between \(y=2x\) and \(y=x^2\).
\begin{bgsolution}
Intersections satisfy \(2x=x^2\), so \(x=0,2\). On \([0,2]\), \(2x\ge x^2\). Therefore
\[
A=\int_0^2(2x-x^2)dx
=\left[x^2-\frac{x^3}{3}\right]_0^2
=\frac43.
\]
\end{bgsolution}
\end{bgguided}

\begin{bgcheck}{u3b-area-01}{rational}{4/3}[Quick check]
Find the area between \(y=2x\) and \(y=x^2\) from \(x=0\) to \(x=2\).
\begin{bghint}Integrate top minus bottom.\end{bghint}
\begin{bgreveal}\[\int_0^2(2x-x^2)dx=4/3.\]\end{bgreveal}
\end{bgcheck}

\section{Choosing Vertical or Horizontal Slices}
\lessonobjective{Choose the orientation that avoids unnecessary piecewise integrals.}

\begin{bgexposition}[title={Choose the orientation that describes the region simply}]
Vertical slices are not automatically superior. Some regions are naturally described by right-minus-left as functions of \(y\), especially when the boundaries are already given as \(x=g(y)\). The orientation should be chosen to make each slice a single segment with a simple length and to avoid unnecessary piecewise formulas.

Draw both a vertical and a horizontal test slice before committing to an integral. Ask which boundaries the slice touches and whether those boundaries change as the slice moves. The differential must match the orientation: vertical slices have thickness \(dx\), horizontal slices have thickness \(dy\). This small planning step often turns a two-integral mess into one clean calculation.
\end{bgexposition}


A region may be simple vertically but piecewise horizontally, or vice versa. Sketch first. Draw one representative slice. Label its length using the axis perpendicular to the slice.

\begin{bgexample}[title={A sideways region}]
The region between \(x=y^2\) and \(x=2y+3\) is naturally described with horizontal slices. Intersections solve \(y^2=2y+3\), giving \(y=-1,3\). Since the line lies to the right,
\[
A=\int_{-1}^{3}[(2y+3)-y^2]dy.
\]
A vertical setup would require solving for \(y\) and splitting the region.
\end{bgexample}

\begin{bgcheck}{u3b-slice-choice-01}{multiple_choice}{horizontal}[Quick check]
For the region bounded by \(x=y^2\) and \(x=y+2\), which slice orientation gives one integral most naturally?
\begin{bghint}Both boundaries are already functions of \(y\).\end{bghint}
\begin{bgreveal}Use horizontal slices.\end{bgreveal}
\end{bgcheck}

\section{Disks and Washers}
\lessonobjective{Construct radius functions and integrate cross-sectional areas for solids of revolution.}

\begin{bgexposition}[title={Radii are distances from the axis of rotation}]
Disks and washers arise from slices perpendicular to the axis of rotation. The radius is not automatically the function value; it is the distance from the axis to the boundary. If the region does not touch the axis, rotation leaves a hole, and the cross-sectional area is outer disk minus inner disk: \(\pi(R^2-r^2)\).

A labeled representative slice prevents most mistakes. Mark the axis, the outer radius, the inner radius, and the thickness before integrating. If the axis is shifted, use distance expressions such as \(f(x)-c\) rather than copying \(f(x)\). Square the radii only after those distances are correct. The geometry decides the formula; the algebra merely carries it out.
\end{bgexposition}


Rotating a region around an axis creates circular cross-sections. A disk has area \(\pi R^2\). A washer has area
\[
\pi(R^2-r^2).
\]

\begin{bgguided}[title={Rotate a region around the x-axis}]
Rotate the region under \(y=\sqrt{x}\) from \(x=0\) to \(x=4\) around the \(x\)-axis.
\begin{bgsolution}
Each vertical slice forms a disk of radius \(R(x)=\sqrt{x}\). Therefore
\[
V=\pi\int_0^4(\sqrt{x})^2dx
=\pi\int_0^4x\,dx=8\pi.
\]
\end{bgsolution}
\end{bgguided}

\begin{bgcheck}{u3b-disk-01}{expression}{8pi}[Quick check]
Find the volume obtained by rotating \(y=\sqrt{x}\), \(0\le x\le4\), around the x-axis.
\begin{bghint}Use disks with radius \(\sqrt{x}\).\end{bghint}
\begin{bgreveal}\[V=\pi\int_0^4x\,dx=8\pi.\]\end{bgreveal}
\end{bgcheck}

\section{Choosing a Volume Method}
\lessonobjective{Choose slices and variables that minimize algebra and interval splitting.}

\begin{bgexposition}[title={Choose the slice before choosing the formula}]
Volume problems become confusing when students begin with a formula instead of a geometric slice. Start by drawing the region and the axis of rotation. Then test a vertical and horizontal strip. A strip perpendicular to the axis produces disks or washers, while a strip parallel to the axis produces shells. The best method is the one that describes the entire solid with the fewest simple pieces.

Method choice should reduce algebra and avoid unnecessary inverse functions. A correct but awkward setup may still be inferior if another orientation gives one clean integral. Record the radius, height, or cross-sectional area directly on the sketch and check units before evaluating. The calculation is usually routine once the geometry has been made explicit; most of the intellectual work occurs before the integral sign appears.
\end{bgexposition}


\begin{bgdecision}[title={Method selection}]
\begin{itemize}
\item Slices perpendicular to the axis of rotation produce disks or washers.
\item Slices parallel to the axis produce shells.
\item Use \(dx\) for vertical slices and \(dy\) for horizontal slices.
\item Sketch one slice and its rotation before writing an integral.
\item Prefer the method that gives one simple integral over several awkward pieces.
\end{itemize}
\end{bgdecision}

\begin{bgcheck}{u3b-volume-choice-01}{multiple_choice}{shells}[Quick check]
A region is described by \(y=f(x)\) and rotated around the y-axis. Which method often avoids solving for x as a function of y?
\begin{bghint}Vertical strips are parallel to the y-axis.\end{bghint}
\begin{bgreveal}Cylindrical shells often preserve the given x-description.\end{bgreveal}
\end{bgcheck}

\section{Surface Area of Revolution}
\lessonobjective{Construct surface-area integrals from circumference times arc-length elements.}

\begin{bgexposition}[title={Surface area combines circumference with slanted length}]
A short piece of curve has length approximately \(ds\). Rotating it around an axis creates a narrow band whose area is approximately circumference times slanted width, \(2\pi r\,ds\). Substituting the arc-length element gives the standard surface-area formula. This explains why surface area includes both a radius factor and a square-root derivative factor.

The radius must be measured from the chosen axis, and the curve must be described without double-counting the surface. Surface-area integrals are sensitive to setup and may be difficult to evaluate exactly. A complete solution should identify the radius, the arc-length factor, the interval, and the units. Comparing simple cases with known cone or cylinder formulas is an excellent check on the model.
\end{bgexposition}


Rotating a short arc element around an axis produces a thin frustum-like band. Around the \(x\)-axis,
\[
S=2\pi\int_a^bf(x)\sqrt{1+[f'(x)]^2}\,dx,
\]
provided \(f(x)\ge0\). The radius must be measured from the axis of rotation.

\begin{bgexample}[title={A cone from a line segment}]
Rotate \(y=x\), \(0\le x\le1\), around the \(x\)-axis:
\[
S=2\pi\int_0^1x\sqrt2\,dx=\pi\sqrt2.
\]
This is the lateral surface area of a cone with radius \(1\) and slant height \(\sqrt2\).
\end{bgexample}

\begin{bgcheck}{u3b-surface-01}{expression}{pi*sqrt(2)}[Quick check]
Find the surface area generated by rotating \(y=x\), \(0\le x\le1\), around the x-axis.
\begin{bghint}Use radius x and arc factor \(\sqrt2\).\end{bghint}
\begin{bgreveal}\[2\pi\int_0^1x\sqrt2dx=\pi\sqrt2.\]\end{bgreveal}
\end{bgcheck}

\section{Density and Mass}
\lessonobjective{Integrate linear density, area density, or volume density to find mass.}

\begin{bgexposition}[title={Density tells how mass is distributed}]
When density is constant, mass is density times size. When density varies, divide the object into small pieces. A short rod segment of length \(dx\) has mass approximately \(\lambda(x)dx\), an area element has mass approximately \(\rho(x)dA\), and a volume element has mass approximately \(\delta(x)dV\). Integration adds the nonuniform contributions.

The form of the density must match the dimension of the object. Linear density has units of mass per length, surface density has mass per area, and volume density has mass per volume. Multiplying by the corresponding differential element should leave units of mass. This unit cancellation is one of the cleanest ways to verify that the model uses the correct density and integration variable.
\end{bgexposition}


For a thin rod along \([a,b]\) with linear density \(\lambda(x)\) mass per length,
\[
m=\int_a^b\lambda(x)\,dx.
\]
The same idea extends to surface and volume density with area or volume elements.

\begin{bgapplication}[title={A rod with increasing density}]
A 3-meter rod has density \(\lambda(x)=2+x\) kilograms per meter. Its mass is
\[
m=\int_0^3(2+x)dx=\frac{21}{2}\text{ kg}.
\]
\end{bgapplication}

\begin{bgcheck}{u3b-mass-01}{rational}{21/2}[Quick check]
A 3-meter rod has density \(2+x\) kg/m. Find its mass.
\begin{bghint}Integrate density over position.\end{bghint}
\begin{bgreveal}\[\int_0^3(2+x)dx=21/2\text{ kg}.\]\end{bgreveal}
\end{bgcheck}

\section{Work and Variable Force}
\lessonobjective{Model work as the integral of force over displacement and interpret units.}

\begin{bgexposition}[title={Work accumulates force through distance}]
For a constant force acting in the direction of motion, work is force times distance. If the force varies with position, divide the displacement into short intervals. On each interval, the force is approximately constant, so the local work is \(F(x_i^*)\Delta x\). The integral \(\int_a^b F(x)\,dx\) is the limiting total.

Signs and units carry physical meaning. Force in the direction of displacement contributes positive work, while an opposing force contributes negative work. In SI units, newtons times meters give joules. A graph of force versus position makes the interpretation visible: work is signed area under the force curve, not area under a position or time graph unless the variables have been transformed appropriately.
\end{bgexposition}


For constant force parallel to motion, \(W=Fd\). For varying force,
\[
W=\int_a^bF(x)\,dx.
\]
In SI units, newtons times meters gives joules.

\begin{bgapplication}[title={A linearly increasing force}]
A force \(F(x)=10+3x\) newtons moves an object from \(x=0\) to \(x=4\) meters:
\[
W=\int_0^4(10+3x)dx=64\text{ J}.
\]
\end{bgapplication}

\begin{bgcheck}{u3b-work-01}{integer}{64}[Quick check]
A force \(F(x)=10+3x\) N moves an object from 0 to 4 m. Find the work.
\begin{bghint}Integrate force with respect to displacement.\end{bghint}
\begin{bgreveal}\[W=[10x+3x^2/2]_0^4=64\text{ J}.\]\end{bgreveal}
\end{bgcheck}

\section{Work Done on Springs}
\lessonobjective{Use Hooke’s law and integrate variable spring force.}

\begin{bgexposition}[title={A spring's force changes with extension}]
Hooke's Law models an ideal spring by \(F(x)=kx\), where \(x\) is displacement from the natural length and \(k\) is the spring constant. Because the required force grows as the spring is stretched or compressed farther, work cannot be found by multiplying one endpoint force by the entire distance. The force must be integrated over the displacement interval.

Be careful about the coordinate used for \(x\). If lengths are measured from a wall or from the spring's total length, first convert them to extension from equilibrium. The work done by an external agent and the work done by the spring have opposite signs, depending on the convention. State which quantity is being computed and include consistent units for \(k\), displacement, and work.
\end{bgexposition}


Hooke's law gives \(F(x)=kx\), where \(x\) is displacement from equilibrium. Work to stretch from \(a\) to \(b\) is
\[
W=\int_a^bkx\,dx=\frac{k}{2}(b^2-a^2).
\]

\begin{bgexample}[title={Calibrate the spring first}]
A 20-newton force stretches a spring \(0.10\) meter. Then \(k=20/0.10=200\) N/m. Work to stretch from equilibrium to \(0.25\) meter is
\[
\int_0^{0.25}200x\,dx=6.25\text{ J}.
\]
\end{bgexample}

\begin{bgcheck}{u3b-spring-01}{decimal}{6.25}[Quick check]
A spring has \(k=200\) N/m. Find the work to stretch it from 0 to 0.25 m.
\begin{bghint}Integrate \(200x\) from 0 to 0.25.\end{bghint}
\begin{bgreveal}\[100(0.25)^2=6.25\text{ J}.\]\end{bgreveal}
\end{bgcheck}

\section{Physics Application Studio}
\begin{bgmodelinglab}[title={A compact set of standard models}]
For each situation, identify the local contribution, variable of integration, bounds, units, and final interpretation before evaluating.
\begin{enumerate}
\item A velocity function changes sign twice. Find displacement and distance.
\item A spring has a measured calibration force and is stretched across a nonzero interval.
\item A rectangular tank is pumped to an outlet above the top.
\item A triangular submerged plate experiences pressure increasing with depth.
\item A nonuniform rod has linear density \(\lambda(x)=a+bx\).
\item A force-position graph is given only as data; estimate work numerically.
\end{enumerate}
\end{bgmodelinglab}
